{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1244003,"path":"axios>follow-redirects","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"1.16.1","paths":["axios>follow-redirects","smartsheet>axios>follow-redirects"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-116689\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/b0a7751e9cd64cd8eeb6ec67d2f206a9cc88d3f8\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/releases/tag/v1.16.2\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","created":"2026-10-30T22:59:33.000Z","id":1244003,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Summary\n\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 validate the documented sensitiveHeaders option with instanceof Array. An array created in another JavaScript realm is silently replaced with an empty list, so a caller-declared custom credential header can be forwarded to an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect destination.\n\n### Details\n\nAffected versions\n\nThe sensitiveHeaders option was introduced in 1.16.0. Published 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 both reproduced, giving a tested affected range of 1.16.0 through 1.16.1. The source checkout was pinned at revision 46301256333c90b7faac7aa126effe72ec16d16e; the exact npm 1.16.1 artifact reproduced independently.\n\nRoot cause\n\nindex.js resets options.sensitiveHeaders when its local isArray() helper returns false. That helper uses value instanceof Array, which depends on realm identity. The same file already uses Array.isArray(options.headers), which correctly recognizes arrays across realms.\n\nSuggested remediation\n\nUse Array.isArray(value) for sensitiveHeaders, then validate and normalize each member as a header-name string. Add same-realm and node:vm cross-realm tests for cross-origin redirects, including the array-form headers representation. Verify that custom sensitive names are removed while unrelated headers remain unchanged.\n\nDuplicate review\n\nThe public advisory history contains other sensitive-header findings, including array-form headers and regular-expression handling, but focused repository searches found no report describing cross-realm validation as of 2026-10-25. A private or differently worded report may already exist.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nStart two loopback HTTP servers. The first returns a 302 redirect to the second. Send X-API-Key and list that name in sensitiveHeaders, then compare a normal array with a genuine cross-realm array created by:\n\n```js\nconst vm = require('node:vm');\nconst sensitiveHeaders = vm.runInNewContext(`['X-API-Key']`);\n```\n\nObserved against both source and the exact npm 1.16.1 package:\n\n- Same-realm ['X-API-Key']: the redirect target did not receive the key.\n- Cross-realm ['X-API-Key']: Array.isArray(value) was true, value instanceof Array was false, and the redirect target received X-API-Key.\n- An unrelated X-Harmless header remained present in both cases.\n\nThe complete 3.5 KB loopback-only harness and raw JSON output are available privately.\n\n### Impact\n\ncustom credential disclosure. Exploitation requires a direct caller or wrapper that preserves a cross-realm sensitiveHeaders array, a custom credential listed in that option, and an attacker-controlled or compromised endpoint that returns a cross-origin redirect. The directly demonstrated result is disclosure of the custom header value, potentially exposing an API key or service token until revocation.\n\nBuilt-in Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, and Cookie protections remained effective. Exact Axios 1.20.0 with follow-redirects 1.16.1 did not reproduce because Axios normalized the array; this report does not claim that Axios is affected. No third-party service was tested.","reported_by":null,"title":"follow-redirects: Cross-realm sensitiveHeaders arrays are discarded and custom credentials can follow redirects","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-116689"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"follow-redirects","vulnerable_versions":">=1.16.0 <=1.16.1","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 1.16.2 or later","patched_versions":">=1.16.2","updated":"2026-10-30T22:59:34.000Z","cvss":{"score":5.9,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N"},"cwe":["CWE-201"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p"}}}
{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1244003,"path":"smartsheet>axios>follow-redirects","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"1.16.1","paths":["axios>follow-redirects","smartsheet>axios>follow-redirects"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/security/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-116689\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/commit/b0a7751e9cd64cd8eeb6ec67d2f206a9cc88d3f8\n- https://github.com/follow-redirects/follow-redirects/releases/tag/v1.16.2\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","created":"2026-10-30T22:59:33.000Z","id":1244003,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Summary\n\nfollow-redirects 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 validate the documented sensitiveHeaders option with instanceof Array. An array created in another JavaScript realm is silently replaced with an empty list, so a caller-declared custom credential header can be forwarded to an attacker-controlled cross-origin redirect destination.\n\n### Details\n\nAffected versions\n\nThe sensitiveHeaders option was introduced in 1.16.0. Published 1.16.0 and 1.16.1 both reproduced, giving a tested affected range of 1.16.0 through 1.16.1. The source checkout was pinned at revision 46301256333c90b7faac7aa126effe72ec16d16e; the exact npm 1.16.1 artifact reproduced independently.\n\nRoot cause\n\nindex.js resets options.sensitiveHeaders when its local isArray() helper returns false. That helper uses value instanceof Array, which depends on realm identity. The same file already uses Array.isArray(options.headers), which correctly recognizes arrays across realms.\n\nSuggested remediation\n\nUse Array.isArray(value) for sensitiveHeaders, then validate and normalize each member as a header-name string. Add same-realm and node:vm cross-realm tests for cross-origin redirects, including the array-form headers representation. Verify that custom sensitive names are removed while unrelated headers remain unchanged.\n\nDuplicate review\n\nThe public advisory history contains other sensitive-header findings, including array-form headers and regular-expression handling, but focused repository searches found no report describing cross-realm validation as of 2026-10-25. A private or differently worded report may already exist.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nStart two loopback HTTP servers. The first returns a 302 redirect to the second. Send X-API-Key and list that name in sensitiveHeaders, then compare a normal array with a genuine cross-realm array created by:\n\n```js\nconst vm = require('node:vm');\nconst sensitiveHeaders = vm.runInNewContext(`['X-API-Key']`);\n```\n\nObserved against both source and the exact npm 1.16.1 package:\n\n- Same-realm ['X-API-Key']: the redirect target did not receive the key.\n- Cross-realm ['X-API-Key']: Array.isArray(value) was true, value instanceof Array was false, and the redirect target received X-API-Key.\n- An unrelated X-Harmless header remained present in both cases.\n\nThe complete 3.5 KB loopback-only harness and raw JSON output are available privately.\n\n### Impact\n\ncustom credential disclosure. Exploitation requires a direct caller or wrapper that preserves a cross-realm sensitiveHeaders array, a custom credential listed in that option, and an attacker-controlled or compromised endpoint that returns a cross-origin redirect. The directly demonstrated result is disclosure of the custom header value, potentially exposing an API key or service token until revocation.\n\nBuilt-in Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, and Cookie protections remained effective. Exact Axios 1.20.0 with follow-redirects 1.16.1 did not reproduce because Axios normalized the array; this report does not claim that Axios is affected. No third-party service was tested.","reported_by":null,"title":"follow-redirects: Cross-realm sensitiveHeaders arrays are discarded and custom credentials can follow redirects","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-116689"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"follow-redirects","vulnerable_versions":">=1.16.0 <=1.16.1","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 1.16.2 or later","patched_versions":">=1.16.2","updated":"2026-10-30T22:59:34.000Z","cvss":{"score":5.9,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N"},"cwe":["CWE-201"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mrwp-f3q8-3g9p"}}}
{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1244007,"path":"joi","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"18.3.0","paths":["joi"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/security/advisories/GHSA-4528-x5fx-h6hg\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-118772\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/pull/3162\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/pull/3163\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/commit/03dc1d909818503b06240dadc81e9227ed2649cf\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/commit/807858d7480f5075b0610817e3e44230890fb18e\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/releases/tag/v17.13.9\n- https://github.com/hapijs/joi/releases/tag/v18.3.1\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4528-x5fx-h6hg","created":"2026-10-30T23:10:23.000Z","id":1244007,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Summary\n\n`Joi.string().email({ multiple: true })` takes quadratic time to split a value that contains a long run of whitespace. One request with 100 KB of spaces blocks the Node.js event loop for several seconds, which causes a denial of service.\n\n### Details\n\nWhen the `multiple` option is set, the `email` rule splits the value into addresses with a regular expression that removes whitespace on each side of the separator:\n\n```js\nconst regex = new RegExp(`\\\\s*[${options.separator ? escapeRegex(options.separator) : ','}]\\\\s*`);\n// ...\nconst emails = options.multiple ? value.split(regex) : [value];\n```\n\n`String.prototype.split` tries a match at each position of the value. At each position inside a whitespace run, the leading `\\s*` reads the rest of the run, does not find a separator, and backtracks through all of it. A run of `n` whitespace characters without a separator therefore costs `n²` steps. This applies to the default separator and to all custom `separator` values.\n\nThe fix lets the leading whitespace match start only at the beginning of a whitespace run, so the split is linear:\n\n```js\nconst regex = new RegExp(`(?:(?<!\\\\s)\\\\s+)?[${options.separator ? escapeRegex(options.separator) : ','}]\\\\s*`);\n```\n\nThe split results do not change. The regular expression is now built only when `multiple` is set.\n\nEvery `joi` release from `17.1.1` up to and including `17.13.8`, and from `18.0.0` up to and including `18.3.0`, is affected. Every `@hapi/joi` release from `16.0.0` is affected.\n\nWorkarounds for users who cannot upgrade:\n\n- Add `.max()` before `.email()`, for example `Joi.string().max(1000).email({ multiple: true })`. The `max` rule then rejects long values before the `email` rule runs.\n- Do not use the `abortEarly: false` option with this schema. With `abortEarly: false`, the `email` rule runs even when the `max` rule fails.\n- Do not use `.trim()` as a workaround. It removes only the whitespace at the start and at the end of the value.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\n```js\nconst Joi = require('joi');\n\nconst schema = Joi.string().email({ multiple: true });\nfor (const n of [25000, 50000, 100000, 200000]) {\n    const start = Date.now();\n    schema.validate(' '.repeat(n));\n    console.log(`${n} spaces: ${Date.now() - start} ms`);\n}\n```\n\nOutput on `joi@18.3.0` (Node.js 26):\n\n```text\n25000 spaces: 244 ms\n50000 spaces: 887 ms\n100000 spaces: 3349 ms\n200000 spaces: 13579 ms\n```\n\nOutput on the patched version:\n\n```text\n25000 spaces: 0 ms\n50000 spaces: 0 ms\n100000 spaces: 0 ms\n200000 spaces: 1 ms\n```\n\nOver HTTP, send the whitespace in a field that the route validates with this schema:\n\n```http\nPOST /invite HTTP/1.1\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"emails\": \"<100000 spaces>\"}\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is a denial of service by CPU exhaustion. It affects each application that validates untrusted input with `Joi.string().email({ multiple: true })` and that has no length limit before the `email` rule. One request with 100 KB of whitespace blocks the Node.js event loop for several seconds, and the server cannot process other requests during that time. The attacker needs no authentication when the route is public, and a small number of concurrent requests can keep the service unavailable.","reported_by":null,"title":"joi: quadratic-time split in `string().email({ multiple: true })` allows denial of service","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-118772"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"joi","vulnerable_versions":">=18.0.0 <18.3.1","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-4528-x5fx-h6hg","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 18.3.1 or later","patched_versions":">=18.3.1","updated":"2026-10-30T23:10:26.000Z","cvss":{"score":7.5,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H"},"cwe":["CWE-1333"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4528-x5fx-h6hg"}}}
